    <<link "Continue" "R5_DaveParkmanEncounter">><</link>>
</div>

:: R5_DaveParkmanEncounter [r5 story intimate]
<<location "Corridor, Floor 39">>
<<objective "Get through the hallway without giving anything away.">>

Dave Parkman catches me in the hallway around 10. Dave is six-two, former lacrosse, the kind of wide-shouldered salesman who backslaps his way through life like personal space is a concept that applies to other people.

<<say "Dave Parkman">>Danny boy!<</say>>

He falls into step beside me.

<<say "Dave Parkman">>You see the Knicks last night? Absolute meltdown in the fourth.<</say>>

<<sayself>>Missed it. Busy night.<</sayself>>

<<say "Dave Parkman">>Brother, you gotta make time.<</say>>

He claps me on the back.

<<moodimage "files/images/mood/r5-dave-backslap.png" "">>

His hand lands square between my shoulder blades. The impact is friendly. Casual. The kind of hit men deliver to other men as punctuation, a physical exclamation point. I've received a thousand of them in my life and never flinched.

I flinch.

His hand landed on the bra band. The clasp dug into my spine from the impact. But worse, so much worse, is the realization that his palm was an inch from the strap. One inch higher and his fingers would have crossed over the strap line running from my shoulder to the band. He would have felt the ridge through my shirt. He would have known.

<<say "Dave Parkman">>You okay? You pull a muscle?<</say>>

<<sayself>>Slept on my neck wrong.<</sayself>>

The lie materializes fully formed. Lies are coming easier now. They arrive before I request them, idling at the curb like a car service I didn't book.

<<say "Dave Parkman">>Sucks. Try a heating pad. My wife swears by hers.<</say>>

He claps me again, lighter this time, on the shoulder. His fingers brush directly over the bra strap.

I feel it. He must feel it. The slight ridge, the strip of thicker fabric beneath the cotton of my shirt. My entire cardiovascular system suspends operations for two seconds.

<<say "Dave Parkman">>See you at lunch?<</say>>

He's already moving. He felt nothing. He noticed nothing. He's thinking about the Knicks and his lunch order and whatever deal he's closing this week. The bra strap under my shirt registered on his fingertips with less significance than a wrinkled collar.

I stand in the hallway and breathe.

The paranoia is not like fear. Fear runs cold. This runs hot. Every interaction is a live wire now, every casual touch a detonation sequence. Somebody might feel. Somebody might know. The possibility crackles under my skin like electricity looking for ground, and the thing I can't explain, the thing that makes my cock stiffen against the lace, is that the electricity feels good. The danger feels good. The secret feels good.

I am standing in a corporate hallway at 10:15 AM with a half-erection in women's underwear because a colleague slapped me on the back and didn't discover my bra. This is my life now. This is what my life is now.

<<modstat "arousalRewiring" 4>>

